  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Partial inland clearing this afternoon gives \nway to returning low clouds and patchy fog late tonight into \nThursday morning. Seasonable temperatures persist through late\nweek.\n\nLatest analysis reveals ~1034 mb surface high pressure centered\noffshore of coastal New England, ridging southwest into the \nlower Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas. Aloft, upper-level ridging \nremains in place across the eastern Gulf coast, extending across\nthe coastal Southeast.\n\nEarly afternoon visible satellite imagery shows persistent \nstratus and stratocumulus lingering across the Northern Neck, \nextending SW into the southwest Virginia Piedmont, mainly south\nof US-360 and west of I-95. Temperatures as of this writing \nremain held down under cloud cover in these areas, with warmer \nconditions in the low to mid 70s and partial sun along the coast.\nLow clouds will briefly scour out into this evening before \nredeveloping overnight, then scouring out again from NE to SW \nThursday morning. Areas of patchy fog are also expected late \ntonight given the light onshore flow, yielding slightly milder \nlows mainly in the 50s. Highs Thursday range from the upper 60s \nacross the western Piedmont to the low and mid-70s elsewhere.\n\nThe surface ridge anchors off the Mid-Atlantic coast Thursday \nand Friday while deepening low pressure remains suppressed near \nthe Bahamas. Offshore high pressure allows low-level winds to \nveer SSW and become breezy Thursday afternoon through Friday. \nThis return flow pushes highs into the mid to upper 70s Friday \nbefore another backdoor cold front drops across the area late \nFriday into Saturday. This boundary knocks temperatures back to \nnear/just above climatological normal for the weekend, with \nhighs in the mid-60s to around 70.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather prevails through Halloween. Rain \nchances late this weekend into early next week continue to trend\ndownward.\n\nA northern stream longwave trough flattens the upper ridge late\nthis week, promoting quasi-zonal flow aloft heading into the \nweekend. Halloween continues to look dry, pleasant, and \nseasonable, albeit with increasing cloud cover as cooler air \nwedges back in from the northeast. This will yield a partly to \nmostly cloudy, cool, and dry Saturday evening, nearly optimal \nconditions for trick- or-treaters.\n\nThereafter, the medium range picture becomes more complex. \nWhile the pattern turns a bit more active next week, latest \nensemble suites continue to struggle with timing/resolving \nprecipitation placement. Guidance can agree that an anomalously \nstrong West Coast upper low shifts east into the Plains by early\nnext week. However, the 12z EPS and its machine-learning AIFS \nhave each trended noticeably drier, or nearly completely dry, \nfor early next week, keeping the bulk of overrunning moisture \nsuppressed to our south. The 12z GEFS and operational GFS have \ntrended toward the rest of the guidance envelope, but remain \nrelatively wet outliers. Thus, while light overrunning rain \nremains possible for late Sunday night into Monday, \nprobabilities for meaningful wetting rainfall continue to trend \ndownward. Some better agreement exists that show additional \nshortwave energy and rain chances arrive toward the middle to \nend of next week as a broader trough develops across the \nSoutheast. That said, given high spread at days 7-10, confidence\nin this remains low at best.",
